<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>9.2.3: Completing the Square</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02354</md:content-id>
    <md:title>9.2.3: Completing the Square</md:title>
    <md:uuid>822d49f5-0fd1-43ba-a63d-13318e113f23</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section>
      <title>Activity</title>
      <para>One technique for solving <term class="glossary-tooltip">quadratic equation</term>s is called <term class="glossary-tooltip">completing the square</term>. Here are two examples of how Diego and Mai completed the square to solve the same equation.</para>
      <table class="os-raise-wideequaltable">
        <tgroup cols="2">
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <entry>
                <emphasis>Diego</emphasis>
              </entry>
              <entry>
                <emphasis>Mai</emphasis>
              </entry>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry>\(\begin{aligned} x^{2}+10 x+9 &amp;=0 \\ x^{2}+10 x &amp;=-9 \\ x^{2}+10 x+25 &amp;=-9+25 \\ x^{2}+10 x+25 &amp;=16 \\ (x+5)^{2} &amp;=16 \\ \sqrt{(x+5)^{2}} &amp;=\sqrt{16} \\ x+5 &amp;=\pm \;4 \\ \\ x+5=4 \text { or } x+5 &amp;=-4 \\ x=-1 \text { and } x=-9 &amp; \end{aligned}\)</entry>
              <entry>\(\begin{aligned} x^{2}+10 x+9 &amp;=0 \\ x^{2}+10 x+9+16 &amp;=16 \\ x^{2}+10 x+25 &amp;=16 \\ (x+5)^{2} &amp;=16 \\ \sqrt{(x+5)^{2}} &amp;=\sqrt{16} \\ (x+5) &amp;=\pm \;4 \\ \\ x+5=4 \text { and } x+5=-4 \\ x=-1 \text { or } x=-9 \end{aligned} \)</entry>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </tgroup>
      </table>
      <para>Study the worked examples. Then, try solving these equations by completing the square for questions 1 – 5.</para>
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>\(x^2+6x+8=0\)</para>
          <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\(\begin{aligned} x^{2}+6 x+8 &amp;=0 \\ x^{2}+6 x &amp;=-8 \\ x^{2}+6 x+9 &amp;=-8+9 \\ (x+3)^{2} &amp;=1 \\ \sqrt{(x+3)^{2}} &amp;=\sqrt{1} \\ x+3 &amp;=\pm \;1 \\ x+3 &amp;=1 \\ x+3 &amp;=-1 \\ \\ x=-4 &amp; \text { and } x=-2 \end{aligned}\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>\(x^2+12x=13\)</para>
          <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\(\begin{aligned} x^{2}+12 x &amp;=13 \\ x^{2}+12 x+36 &amp;=13+36 \\ x^{2}+12 x+36 &amp;=49 \\ (x+6)^{2} &amp;=49 \\ \sqrt{(x+6)^{2}} &amp;=\sqrt{49} \\ x+6 &amp;=\pm \;7 \\ x+6 &amp;=7 \\ x+6 &amp;=-7 \\ \\ x=-13 &amp; \text { and } x=1 \end{aligned}\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>\(0=x^2−10x+21\)</para>
          <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\(\begin{aligned} 0=\; &amp; x^{2}-10 x+21 \\ x^{2}-10 x+21 &amp;=0 \\ x^{2}-10 x &amp;=-21 \\ x^{2}-10 x+25 &amp;=-21+25 \\ x^{2}-10 x+25 &amp;=4 \\ (x-5)^{2} &amp;=4 \\ \sqrt{(x-5)^{2}} &amp;=\sqrt{4} \\ x-5 &amp;=\pm \;2 \\ x-5 &amp;=2 \\ x-5 &amp;=-2 \\ \\ x=3 &amp; \text { and } x=7 \end{aligned}\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>\(x^2−2x+3=83\)</para>
          <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\(\begin{aligned} x^{2}-2 x+3 &amp;=83 \\ x^{2}-2 x &amp;=83-3 \\ x^{2}-2 x &amp;=80 \\ x^{2}-2 x+1 &amp;=80+1 \\ x^{2}-2 x+1 &amp;=81 \\ \sqrt{(x-1)^{2}} &amp;=\sqrt{81} \\ x-1 &amp;=\pm \;9 \\ x-1 &amp;=9 \\ x-1 &amp;=-9 \\ \\ x=-8 &amp; \text { and } x=10 \end{aligned}\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>\(x^2+40=14x\)</para>
          <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\(\begin{aligned} x^{2}+40 &amp;=14 x \\ x^{2}-14 x+40 &amp;=0 \\ x^{2}-14 x &amp;=-40 \\ x^{2}-14 x+49 &amp;=-40+49 \\ x^{2}-14 x+49 &amp;=9 \\ \sqrt{(x-7)^{2}} &amp;=\sqrt{9} \\ x-7 &amp;=\pm \;3 \\ x-7 &amp;=3 \\ x-7 &amp;=-3 \\ \\ x=4 &amp; \text { and } x=10 \end{aligned}\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <section>
        <title>Are you ready for more?</title>
        <exercise class="RFM1">
          <problem>
            <title>Extending Your Thinking</title>
            <para>Here is a diagram made of a square and two congruent rectangles. Its total area is \(x^2+35x\) square units.</para>
            <para>Diagram A</para>
            <para>
              <figure>
                <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
                  <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_10_04_06_01.svg" />
                </media>
              </figure>
            </para>
            <para>What is the length of the unlabeled side of each of the two rectangles in Diagram A?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Enter the length of the unlabeled side of each of the two rectangles.</para>
            <para>Compare your answer:</para>
            <para>17.5</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <!--End interaction-->
        <exercise class="RFM1">
          <problem>
            <para>In Diagram B, we add lines to make the figure a square. The area of the square becomes a quadratic equation.</para>
            <para>
              <figure>
                <media alt="">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_10_04_06_02.jpg" />
                </media>
              </figure>
            </para>
            <para>Calculate the equation, then put that equation in the standard form \(ax^2+bx+c\) to answer questions a and b.</para>
            <para>Using the standard form \(ax^2+bx+c\) to represent the area of the square in Diagram B, what is the value of the coefficient \(b\)? </para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Enter the value of the coefficient \(b\).</para>
            <para>Compare your answer:</para>
            <para>35</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <!--End interaction-->
        <exercise class="RFM1">
          <problem>
            <para>Using the standard form \(ax^2+bx+c\) to represent the area of the square in Diagram B, what is the value of the constant \(c\)? </para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Enter the value of the constant \(c\).</para>
            <para>Compare your answer:</para>
            <para>306.25</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <!--End interaction-->
        <exercise class="RFM1">
          <problem>
            <para>How is the process of finding the area of the entire figure like the process of building perfect squares for expressions like \(x^2+bx\)?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Enter your explanation of how the two processes are alike.</para>
            <para>Compare your answer:</para>
            <para>First, we had to figure out what number was half of 35 (which is 17.5) to find the side length of the rectangles. Then, we had to square the number 17.5 to find the area of the missing piece.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
      <!--End interaction-->
      <section>
        <title>Video: Solve Equations by Completing the Square</title>
